                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h1]Played On: [b]HTC Vive[/b][/h1]\n\nKiya is a short, five minute retelling of a tragic domestic violence dispute that took place on June 18, 2013 in Charleston, South Carolina. Without going in on too great of detail, two sisters attempt to rescue their third sister, who is being held captive in an apartment by her jealous and deranged ex-boyfriend. Instead of using voice actors, the game designers chose to use two recorded 911 emergency calls, to which the animations play out around.\n\nAs other reviewers have pointed out, the graphics are less than amazing, almost rivaling late Playstation 2 era quality. However, the visuals are not the point of Kiya, and the experience as a whole brings to light an issue that goes widely unaddressed and under-reported. The disturbing subject matter is something that really hits home with me, as even living in Canada I've dealt with deadly domestic violence situations close to where I live; in fact, just a couple years ago a jealous ex-boyfriend murdered a woman that lived just across the street from me, leaving her three children parentless after he turned the gun on himself afterward.\n\nKiya definitely shines a bright spotlight on the sad issues surrounding domestic violence. So many children have been left without a parent, or parents, and so many families have lost sisters, brothers, uncles, cousins, aunts, and uncles to senseless acts of violence. The decision to use live 911 calls is a great one, as it brings true human emotions to the forefront of the event, and invokes the emotions of the viewer. It's worth checking out if you have five minutes to spare, but don't expect a graphical masterpiece.\n\n[h1]Rating: [b]3.0/5.0[/b] - Average, can take it or leave it.[/h1]\nThe VR Network [url=https://store.steampowered.com/curator/33182845-The-VR-Network/]Curator[/url] | [url=https://steamcommunity.com/groups/thevrnetwork]Group[/url] Click for More",
